{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03788","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03788","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03788","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abukar","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Abukar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lightweight and Data-Efficient MultivariateTime Series Forecasting using Residual-Stacked Gaussian (RS-GLinear) Architecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:44:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Following the success of Transformer architectures in language modeling, particularly their ability to capture long-range dependencies, researchers have explored how these architectures can be adapted for time-series forecasting. Transformer-based models have been proposed to handle both short- and long-term dependencies when predicting future values from historical data. However, studies such as those by Zeng et al. (2022) and Rizvi et al. (2025) have reported mixed results in long-term forecasting tasks. In this work, we evaluate the Gaussian-based Linear architecture introduced by Rizvi et al. (2025) and present an enhanced version called the Residual Stacked Gaussian Linear (RSGL) model. We also investigate the broader applicability of the RSGL model in additional domains, including financial time series and epidemiological data. Experimental results show that the RSGL model achieves improved prediction accuracy and robustness compared to both the baseline Gaussian Linear and Transformer-based models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03788","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03781","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03781","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03781","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majid","familyName":"Asgari-Bidhendi","name":"Asgari-Bidhendi, Majid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Amin","familyName":"Ghaseminia","name":"Ghaseminia, Muhammad Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Shahbazi","name":"Shahbazi, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayyed Ali","familyName":"Hossayni","name":"Hossayni, Sayyed Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Najmeh","familyName":"Torabian","name":"Torabian, Najmeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Behrouz","familyName":"Minaei-Bidgoli","name":"Minaei-Bidgoli, Behrouz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rezwan: Leveraging Large Language Models for Comprehensive Hadith Text Processing: A 1.2M Corpus Development"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:09:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the development of Rezwan, a large-scale AI-assisted Hadith corpus comprising over 1.2M narrations, extracted and structured through a fully automated pipeline. Building on digital repositories such as Maktabat Ahl al-Bayt, the pipeline employs Large Language Models (LLMs) for segmentation, chain--text separation, validation, and multi-layer enrichment. Each narration is enhanced with machine translation into twelve languages, intelligent diacritization, abstractive summarization, thematic tagging, and cross-text semantic analysis. This multi-step process transforms raw text into a richly annotated research-ready infrastructure for digital humanities and Islamic studies. A rigorous evaluation was conducted on 1,213 randomly sampled narrations, assessed by six domain experts. Results show near-human accuracy in structured tasks such as chain--text separation (9.33/10) and summarization (9.33/10), while highlighting ongoing challenges in diacritization and semantic similarity detection. Comparative analysis against the manually curated Noor Corpus demonstrates the superiority of Najm in both scale and quality, with a mean overall score of 8.46/10 versus 3.66/10. Furthermore, cost analysis confirms the economic feasibility of the AI approach: tasks requiring over 229,000 hours of expert labor were completed within months at a fraction of the cost. The work introduces a new paradigm in religious text processing by showing how AI can augment human expertise, enabling large-scale, multilingual, and semantically enriched access to Islamic heritage."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03781","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03780","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03780","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03780","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqiao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yiqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Benchmark Study of Deep Learning Methods for Multi-Label Pediatric Electrocardiogram-Based Cardiovascular Disease Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T11:08:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:32:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cardiovascular disease (CVD) is a major pediatric health burden, and early screening is of critical importance. Electrocardiography (ECG), as a noninvasive and accessible tool, is well suited for this purpose. This paper presents the first benchmark study of deep learning for multi-label pediatric CVD classification on the recently released ZZU-pECG dataset, comprising 3716 recordings with 19 CVD categories. We systematically evaluate four representative paradigms--ResNet-1D, BiLSTM, Transformer, and Mamba 2--under both 9-lead and 12-lead configurations. All models achieved strong results, with Hamming Loss as low as 0.0069 and F1-scores above 85% in most settings. ResNet-1D reached a macro-F1 of 94.67% on the 12-lead subset, while BiLSTM and Transformer also showed competitive performance. Per-class analysis indicated challenges for rare conditions such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in the 9-lead subset, reflecting the effect of limited positive samples. This benchmark establishes reusable baselines and highlights complementary strengths across paradigms. It further points to the need for larger-scale, multi-center validation, age-stratified analysis, and broader disease coverage to support real-world pediatric ECG applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03780","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03771","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03771","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03771","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divij","familyName":"Handa","name":"Handa, Divij","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Blincoe","name":"Blincoe, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Orson","familyName":"Adams","name":"Adams, Orson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinlin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yinlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OptAgent: Optimizing Query Rewriting for E-commerce via Multi-Agent Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T10:41:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:31:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deploying capable and user-aligned LLM-based systems necessitates reliable evaluation. While LLMs excel in verifiable tasks like coding and mathematics, where gold-standard solutions are available, adoption remains challenging for subjective tasks that lack a single correct answer. E-commerce Query Rewriting (QR) is one such problem where determining whether a rewritten query properly captures the user intent is extremely difficult to figure out algorithmically. In this work, we introduce OptAgent, a novel framework that combines multi-agent simulations with genetic algorithms to verify and optimize queries for QR. Instead of relying on a static reward model or a single LLM judge, our approach uses multiple LLM-based agents, each acting as a simulated shopping customer, as a dynamic reward signal. The average of these agent-derived scores serves as an effective fitness function for an evolutionary algorithm that iteratively refines the user's initial query. We evaluate OptAgent on a dataset of 1000 real-world e-commerce queries in five different categories, and we observe an average improvement of 21.98% over the original user query and 3.36% over a Best-of-N LLM rewriting baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03771","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03762","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03762","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03762","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deshan","familyName":"Sumanathilaka","name":"Sumanathilaka, Deshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Micallef","name":"Micallef, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Hough","name":"Hough, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Balance Matters: Understanding How Imbalanced Few-Shot Learning Affects Multilingual Sense Disambiguation in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T10:07:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:30:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have significantly reshaped the landscape of Natural Language Processing (NLP). Among the various prompting techniques, few-shot prompting has gained considerable attention for its practicality and effectiveness. This study investigates how few-shot prompting strategies impact the Word Sense Disambiguation (WSD) task, particularly focusing on the biases introduced by imbalanced sample distributions. We use the GLOSSGPT prompting method, an advanced approach for English WSD, to test its effectiveness across five languages: English, German, Spanish, French, and Italian. Our results show that imbalanced few-shot examples can cause incorrect sense predictions in multilingual languages, but this issue does not appear in English. To assess model behavior, we evaluate both the GPT-4o and LLaMA-3.1-70B models and the results highlight the sensitivity of multilingual WSD to sample distribution in few-shot settings, emphasizing the need for balanced and representative prompting strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper accepted at GlobalNLP 2025: Workshop on beyond English: Natural Language Processing for All Languages in an Era of Large Language Models\" 9 pages, 3 figures, 2 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03762","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03760","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03760","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03760","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingfu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qingfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvoEngineer: Mastering Automated CUDA Kernel Code Evolution with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T10:00:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:30:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CUDA kernel optimization has become a critical bottleneck for AI performance, as deep learning training and inference efficiency directly depends on highly optimized GPU kernels.\n Despite the promise of Large Language Models (LLMs) for automating kernel optimization, this field suffers from a fragmented ecosystem of isolated and incomparable approaches with unclear problem formulations.\n Furthermore, general-purpose LLM code evolution methods cannot meet strict correctness requirements of CUDA kernel optimization.\n We address these fundamental challenges by first formalizing CUDA kernel optimization as a code optimization task with a clear objective, constraints, and evaluation metrics.\n We then establish the first systematic LLM-based code evolution framework, EvoEngineer, that provides guidance for designing and adapting optimization strategies to achieve a balance between performance and correctness.\n Finally, we implement a kernel optimization system based on this framework and conduct extensive experiments on 91 real-world CUDA kernels.\n Our results demonstrate that EvoEngineer achieves a principled balance between performance and correctness, with the highest averaged median speedup of \\textbf{2.72}$\\times$ over baseline CUDA kernels and a code validity rate of \\textbf{69.8}\\%, outperforming existing methods on both dimensions.\n Our method achieves a maximum speedup of \\textbf{36.75}$\\times$ among all operations over PyTorch kernels and delivers the highest speedup on \\textbf{28} (\\textbf{56.0\\%}) of 50 operations that achieve over \\textbf{2$\\times$} acceleration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review of ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03760","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03748","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03748","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03748","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramtin","familyName":"Kakavand","name":"Kakavand, Ramtin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ebrahim","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Ebrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TreePrompt: Leveraging Hierarchical Few-Shot Example Selection for Improved English-Persian and English-German Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T09:26:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:30:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have consistently demonstrated strong performance in machine translation, especially when guided by high-quality prompts. Few-shot prompting is an effective technique to improve translation quality; however, most existing example selection methods focus solely on query-to-example similarity and do not account for the quality of the examples. In this work, we propose TreePrompt, a novel example selection approach that learns LLM preferences to identify high-quality, contextually relevant examples within a tree-structured framework. To further explore the balance between similarity and quality, we combine TreePrompt with K-Nearest Neighbors (K-NN) and Adaptive Few-Shot Prompting (AFSP). Evaluations on two language pairs - English-Persian (MIZAN) and English-German (WMT19) - show that integrating TreePrompt with AFSP or Random selection leads to improved translation performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03748","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03744","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03744","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03744","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianfei","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Qianfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Peijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wensheng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Wensheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meie","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Meie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HydroFusion-LMF: Semi-Supervised Multi-Network Fusion with Large-Model Adaptation for Long-Term Daily Runoff Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Geophysics (physics.geo-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T09:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate decade-scale daily runoff forecasting in small watersheds is difficult because signals blend drifting trends, multi-scale seasonal cycles, regime shifts, and sparse extremes. Prior deep models (DLinear, TimesNet, PatchTST, TiDE, Nonstationary Transformer, LSTNet, LSTM) usually target single facets and under-utilize unlabeled spans, limiting regime adaptivity. We propose HydroFusion-LMF, a unified framework that (i) performs a learnable trend-seasonal-residual decomposition to reduce non-stationarity, (ii) routes residuals through a compact heterogeneous expert set (linear refinement, frequency kernel, patch Transformer, recurrent memory, dynamically normalized attention), (iii) fuses expert outputs via a hydrologic context-aware gate conditioned on day-of-year phase, antecedent precipitation, local variance, flood indicators, and static basin attributes, and (iv) augments supervision with a semi-supervised multi-task objective (composite MSE/MAE + extreme emphasis + NSE/KGE, masked reconstruction, multi-scale contrastive alignment, augmentation consistency, variance-filtered pseudo-labeling). Optional adapter / LoRA layers inject a frozen foundation time-series encoder efficiently. On a ~10-year daily dataset HydroFusion-LMF attains MSE 1.0128 / MAE 0.5818, improving the strongest baseline (DLinear) by 10.2% / 10.3% and the mean baseline by 24.6% / 17.1%. We observe simultaneous MSE and MAE reductions relative to baselines. The framework balances interpretability (explicit components, sparse gating) with performance, advancing label-efficient hydrologic forecasting under non-stationarity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"V1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03744","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zachary","familyName":"Eberhart","name":"Eberhart, Zachary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Collin","familyName":"McMillan","name":"McMillan, Collin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"APIDA-Chat: Structured Synthesis of API Search Dialogues to Bootstrap Conversational Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T08:58:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-language-model assistants are suitable for explaining popular APIs, yet they falter on niche or proprietary libraries because the multi-turn dialogue data needed for fine-tuning are scarce. We present APIDA-Chat, an open-source pipeline that converts symbolic dialogue-act \"scripts\" into realistic, domain-grounded API Search conversations using a lightweight model for inexpensive training data generation. Phase I pairs a legacy dialogue planner with a high-capability teacher LLM (o4-mini) to synthesize a \"gold set\" of realized dialogues; then, a smaller Llama 3.2 3B student model is fine-tuned on this corpus. Phase II drops the teacher and reuses the same planner with the fine-tuned model, allowing rapid, low-cost synthesis of new dialogues without exposing source code to external services. The fine-tuned student improves BLEU from 0.38 to 0.50 and BERTScore from 0.88 to 0.91 versus the base model while running entirely on a single consumer GPU. All components are modular and publicly released to serve as a conservative baseline for future work. APIDA-Chat is open-sourced at https://github.com/Zeberhart/apida-chat and a video demo is available at https://youtu.be/YqmZBHyGbPs ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages, 2 figures. To be published in Proceedings of the 40th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Automated Software Engineering"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:57:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:57:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:57:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03719","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03719","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03719","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Griffin","familyName":"Pitts","name":"Pitts, Griffin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anurata Prabha","familyName":"Hridi","name":"Hridi, Anurata Prabha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun-Balajiee","familyName":"Lekshmi-Narayanan","name":"Lekshmi-Narayanan, Arun-Balajiee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey of LLM-Based Applications in Programming Education: Balancing Automation and Human Oversight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T07:46:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:27:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Novice programmers benefit from timely, personalized support that addresses individual learning gaps, yet the availability of instructors and teaching assistants is inherently limited. Large language models (LLMs) present opportunities to scale such support, though their effectiveness depends on how well technical capabilities are aligned with pedagogical goals. This survey synthesizes recent work on LLM applications in programming education across three focal areas: formative code feedback, assessment, and knowledge modeling. We identify recurring design patterns in how these tools are applied and find that interventions are most effective when educator expertise complements model output through human-in-the-loop oversight, scaffolding, and evaluation. Fully automated approaches are often constrained in capturing the pedagogical nuances of programming education, although human-in-the-loop designs and course specific adaptation offer promising directions for future improvement. Future research should focus on improving transparency, strengthening alignment with pedagogy, and developing systems that flexibly adapt to the needs of varied learning contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2025 EMNLP HCI+NLP Workshop Short Paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03719","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:56:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:56:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:56:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03705","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03705","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03705","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Hooi","name":"Hooi, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T07:11:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:26:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the development of technology, large language models (LLMs) have dominated the downstream natural language processing (NLP) tasks. However, because of the LLMs' instruction-following abilities and inability to distinguish the instructions in the data content, such as web pages from search engines, the LLMs are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. These attacks trick the LLMs into deviating from the original input instruction and executing the attackers' target instruction. Recently, various instruction hierarchy defense strategies are proposed to effectively defend against prompt injection attacks via fine-tuning. In this paper, we explore more vicious attacks that nullify the prompt injection defense methods, even the instruction hierarchy: backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks, where the attackers utilize the backdoor attack for prompt injection attack purposes. Specifically, the attackers poison the supervised fine-tuning samples and insert the backdoor into the model. Once the trigger is activated, the backdoored model executes the injected instruction surrounded by the trigger. We construct a benchmark for comprehensive evaluation. Our experiments demonstrate that backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks are more harmful than previous prompt injection attacks, nullifying existing prompt injection defense methods, even the instruction hierarchy techniques."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03705","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:56:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:56:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:56:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungseop","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Seungseop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gibaeg","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Gibaeg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunkyung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hyunkyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wooseok","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wooseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyo","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Jaehyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunho","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Eunho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"H-DDx: A Hierarchical Evaluation Framework for Differential Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T06:42:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:25:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An accurate differential diagnosis (DDx) is essential for patient care, shaping therapeutic decisions and influencing outcomes. Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as promising tools to support this process by generating a DDx list from patient narratives. However, existing evaluations of LLMs in this domain primarily rely on flat metrics, such as Top-k accuracy, which fail to distinguish between clinically relevant near-misses and diagnostically distant errors. To mitigate this limitation, we introduce H-DDx, a hierarchical evaluation framework that better reflects clinical relevance. H-DDx leverages a retrieval and reranking pipeline to map free-text diagnoses to ICD-10 codes and applies a hierarchical metric that credits predictions closely related to the ground-truth diagnosis. In benchmarking 22 leading models, we show that conventional flat metrics underestimate performance by overlooking clinically meaningful outputs, with our results highlighting the strengths of domain-specialized open-source models. Furthermore, our framework enhances interpretability by revealing hierarchical error patterns, demonstrating that LLMs often correctly identify the broader clinical context even when the precise diagnosis is missed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"GenAI4Health @NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:56:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:56:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:56:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03696","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03696","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03696","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepak Babu","familyName":"Piskala","name":"Piskala, Deepak Babu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharlene","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Sharlene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Udita","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Udita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parul","familyName":"Kalra","name":"Kalra, Parul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Castrillo","name":"Castrillo, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mind the Goal: Data-Efficient Goal-Oriented Evaluation of Conversational Agents and Chatbots using Teacher Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T06:22:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:25:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating the quality of multi-turn chatbot interactions remains challenging, as most existing methods assess interactions at the turn level without addressing whether a user's overarching goal was fulfilled. A ``goal'' here refers to an information need or task, such as asking for policy information or applying for leave. We propose a comprehensive framework for goal-oriented evaluation of multi-agent systems (MAS), introducing the \\textbf{Goal Success Rate (GSR)} to measure the percentage of fulfilled goals, and a \\textbf{Root Cause of Failure (RCOF)} taxonomy to identify reasons for failure in multi-agent chatbots. Our method segments conversations by user goals and evaluates success using all relevant turns. We present a model-based evaluation system combining teacher LLMs, where domain experts define goals, set quality standards serving as a guidance for the LLMs. The LLMs use ``thinking tokens'' to produce interpretable rationales, enabling \\textit{explainable}, \\textit{data-efficient} evaluations. In an enterprise setting, we apply our framework to evaluate AIDA, a zero-to-one employee conversational agent system built as a ground-up multi-agent conversational agent, and observe GSR improvement from 63\\% to 79\\% over six months since its inception. Our framework is generic and offers actionable insights through a detailed defect taxonomy based on analysis of failure points in multi-agent chatbots, diagnosing overall success, identifying key failure modes, and informing system improvements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03696","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:56:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:56:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:56:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehua","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojin","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Xiaojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xiongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REG: A Regularization Optimizer for Robust Training Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T06:05:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimizers are crucial for the efficient training of Large Language Models (LLMs). While AdamW is the de facto standard, recent structure-aware optimizers like Muon have emerged, which regularize gradient updates by operating on entire weight matrices. The Muon optimizer balances the gradient updates along all the directions. However, Muon's reliance on the matrix sign function can lead to training instability, exhibits incompatibility when fine-tuning models pre-trained with AdamW. To address these limitations, we propose \\textbf{REG}, a novel optimizer that replaces Muon's aggressive matrix sign operator with the Row-and-Column-Scaling (RACS) operator. Theoretically grounded in balancing a matrix, the RACS operator regularizes the update steps in a less drastic manner, making it simpler to implement and more compatible with established training dynamics. Through extensive empirical experiments on LLM training, we demonstrate that our REG optimizer not only achieves superior performance and stability over AdamW, but also maintains consistency with the AdamW training paradigm. This consistency is particularly evident during the fine-tuning stage, where REG optimizer avoids the performance degradation observed with Muon."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:56:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:56:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:56:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadjavad","familyName":"Abbaspour","name":"Abbaspour, Mohammadjavad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mukund R.","familyName":"Shukla","name":"Shukla, Mukund R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praveen K.","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Praveen K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivam","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Shivam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Energy Management in Indoor Farming Using Lighting Flexibility and Intelligent Model Predictive Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T06:00:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:24:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Indoor farming enables year-round food production but its reliance on artificial lighting significantly increases energy consumption, peak load charges, and energy costs for growers. Recent studies indicate that plants are able to tolerate interruptions in light, enabling the design of 24-hour lighting schedules (or \"recipes\") with strategic light modulation in alignment with day-ahead pricing. Thus, we propose an optimal lighting control strategy for indoor farming that modulates light intensity and photoperiod to reduce energy costs. The control strategy is implemented within a model predictive control framework and augmented with transformer-based neural networks to forecast 24-hour ahead solar radiation and electricity prices to improve energy cost reduction. The control strategy is informed by real-world experimentation on lettuce crops to discover minimum light exposure and appropriate dark-light intervals, which are mathematically formulated as constraints to maintain plant health. Simulations for a one-hectare greenhouse, based on real electricity market data from Ontario, demonstrate an annual cost reduction of $318,400 (20.9%), a peak load decrease of 1.6 MW (33.32%), and total energy savings of 1890 MWh (20.2%) against a baseline recipe. These findings highlight the potential of intelligent lighting control to improve the sustainability and economic feasibility of indoor farming."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:55:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bumjun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Bumjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjae","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Dongjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dueun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dueun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonje","familyName":"Jeung","name":"Jeung, Wonje","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"No","name":"No, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rainbow Padding: Mitigating Early Termination in Instruction-Tuned Diffusion LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T05:24:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:23:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) have emerged as a promising alternative to autoregressive models, offering flexible generation orders and strong performance on complex reasoning tasks. However, instruction-tuned dLLMs exhibit a critical vulnerability we term \\texttt{} overflow: as allocated sequence length increases, responses paradoxically become shorter, collapsing into early termination or degenerating into streams of \\texttt{} tokens. Although noticed in practice, this issue has not been systematically analyzed. We trace its root cause to the dual role of \\texttt{} as both termination and padding, which concentrates probability mass on \\texttt{} at later positions and propagates backward to trigger early termination. To address this, we introduce Rainbow Padding, a simple remedy that replaces repeated \\texttt{} placeholders with a repeating cycle of distinct padding tokens, distributing probability mass and breaking \\texttt{} dominance. Experiments show that Rainbow Padding substantially improves length robustness and output quality, with as few as seven padding tokens sufficient to prevent early termination. Moreover, the method integrates efficiently into existing instruction-tuned models: LoRA fine-tuning for a single epoch on minimal data yields significant improvements, making this solution highly practical. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/quasar529/rainbow-padding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages. Project page available at~\\url{https://ai-isl.github.io/rainbow-padding}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:55:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenghao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Shenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samson","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Samson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Sampling Data Structures for Tensor Products in Turnstile Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T05:16:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper studies the computational challenges of large-scale attention-based models in artificial intelligence by utilizing importance sampling methods in the streaming setting. Inspired by the classical definition of the $\\ell_2$ sampler and the recent progress of the attention scheme in Large Language Models (LLMs), we propose the definition of the attention sampler. Our approach significantly reduces the computational burden of traditional attention mechanisms. We analyze the effectiveness of the attention sampler from a theoretical perspective, including space and update time. Additionally, our framework exhibits scalability and broad applicability across various model architectures and domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:55:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jijie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloofar","familyName":"Mireshghallah","name":"Mireshghallah, Niloofar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Operationalizing Data Minimization for Privacy-Preserving LLM Prompting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:20:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:22:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid deployment of large language models (LLMs) in consumer applications has led to frequent exchanges of personal information. To obtain useful responses, users often share more than necessary, increasing privacy risks via memorization, context-based personalization, or security breaches. We present a framework to formally define and operationalize data minimization: for a given user prompt and response model, quantifying the least privacy-revealing disclosure that maintains utility, and we propose a priority-queue tree search to locate this optimal point within a privacy-ordered transformation space. We evaluated the framework on four datasets spanning open-ended conversations (ShareGPT, WildChat) and knowledge-intensive tasks with single-ground-truth answers (CaseHold, MedQA), quantifying achievable data minimization with nine LLMs as the response model. Our results demonstrate that larger frontier LLMs can tolerate stronger data minimization while maintaining task quality than smaller open-source models (85.7% redaction for GPT-5 vs. 19.3% for Qwen2.5-0.5B). By comparing with our search-derived benchmarks, we find that LLMs struggle to predict optimal data minimization directly, showing a bias toward abstraction that leads to oversharing. This suggests not just a privacy gap, but a capability gap: models may lack awareness of what information they actually need to solve a task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:55:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03659","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03659","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03659","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Benyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Difan","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Difan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Does higher interpretability imply better utility? A Pairwise Analysis on Sparse Autoencoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T04:14:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:22:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs) are widely used to steer large language models (LLMs), based on the assumption that their interpretable features naturally enable effective model behavior steering. Yet, a fundamental question remains unanswered: does higher interpretability indeed imply better steering utility? To answer this question, we train 90 SAEs across three LLMs (Gemma-2-2B, Qwen-2.5-3B, Gemma-2-9B), spanning five architectures and six sparsity levels, and evaluate their interpretability and steering utility based on SAEBench (arXiv:2501.12345) and AxBench (arXiv:2502.23456) respectively, and perform a rank-agreement analysis via Kendall's rank coefficients (tau b). Our analysis reveals only a relatively weak positive association (tau b approx 0.298), indicating that interpretability is an insufficient proxy for steering performance. We conjecture the interpretability utility gap may stem from the selection of SAE features, as not all of them are equally effective for steering. To further find features that truly steer the behavior of LLMs, we propose a novel selection criterion called Delta Token Confidence, which measures how much amplifying a feature changes the next token distribution. We show that our method improves the steering performance of three LLMs by 52.52 percent compared to the current best output score based criterion (arXiv:2503.34567). Strikingly, after selecting features with high Delta Token Confidence, the correlation between interpretability and utility vanishes (tau b approx 0), and can even become negative. This further highlights the divergence between interpretability and utility for the most effective steering features."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03659","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:55:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Sadikov","name":"Sadikov, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Guided Evolutionary Program Synthesis for Quasi-Monte Carlo Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T03:32:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-discrepancy point sets and digital sequences underpin quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC) methods for high-dimensional integration. We cast two long-standing QMC design problems as program synthesis and solve them with an LLM-guided evolutionary loop that mutates and selects code under task-specific fitness: (i) constructing finite 2D/3D point sets with low star discrepancy, and (ii) choosing Sobol' direction numbers that minimize randomized QMC error on downstream integrands. Our two-phase procedure combines constructive code proposals with iterative numerical refinement. On finite sets, we rediscover known optima in small 2D cases and set new best-known 2D benchmarks for N \u0026gt;= 40, while matching most known 3D optima up to the proven frontier (N \u0026lt;= 8) and reporting improved 3D benchmarks beyond. On digital sequences, evolving Sobol' parameters yields consistent reductions in randomized quasi-Monte Carlo (rQMC) mean-squared error for several 32-dimensional option-pricing tasks relative to widely used Joe--Kuo parameters, while preserving extensibility to any sample size and compatibility with standard randomizations. Taken together, the results demonstrate that LLM-driven evolutionary program synthesis can automate the discovery of high-quality QMC constructions, recovering classical designs where they are optimal and improving them where finite-N structure matters. Data and code are available at https://github.com/hockeyguy123/openevolve-star-discrepancy.git."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:55:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:55:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:55:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Masuda","name":"Masuda, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Kouzawa","name":"Kouzawa, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyousuke","familyName":"Sezai","name":"Sezai, Kyousuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hidetoshi","familyName":"Suhara","name":"Suhara, Hidetoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuaki","familyName":"Hiruta","name":"Hiruta, Yasuaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunihiro","familyName":"Kudou","name":"Kudou, Kunihiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating High-Level Test Cases from Requirements using LLM: An Industry Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T03:05:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:20:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Currently, generating high-level test cases described in natural language from requirement documents is performed manually. In the industry, including companies specializing in software testing, there is a significant demand for the automatic generation of high-level test cases from requirement documents using Large Language Models (LLMs). Efforts to utilize LLMs for requirement analysis are underway. In some cases, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is employed for generating high-level test cases using LLMs. However, in practical applications, it is necessary to create a RAG tailored to the knowledge system of each specific application, which is labor-intensive. Moreover, when applying high-level test case generation as a prompt, there is no established method for instructing the generation of high-level test cases at a level applicable to other specifications without using RAG. It is required to establish a method for the automatic generation of high-level test cases that can be generalized across a wider range of requirement documents. In this paper, we propose a method for generating high-level (GHL) test cases from requirement documents using only prompts, without creating RAGs. In the proposed method, first, the requirement document is input into the LLM to generate test design techniques corresponding to the requirement document. Then, high-level test cases are generated for each of the generated test design techniques. Furthermore, we verify an evaluation method based on semantic similarity of the generated high-level test cases. In the experiments, we confirmed the method using datasets from Bluetooth and Mozilla, where requirement documents and high-level test cases are available, achieving macro-recall measurement of 0.81 and 0.37, respectively. We believe that the method is feasible for practical application in generating high-level test cases without using RAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:54:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03636","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03636","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03636","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rabeya Amin","familyName":"Jhuma","name":"Jhuma, Rabeya Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostafa Mohaimen Akand","familyName":"Faisal","name":"Faisal, Mostafa Mohaimen Akand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Theory to Practice: Evaluating Data Poisoning Attacks and Defenses in In-Context Learning on Social Media Health Discourse"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T02:47:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:19:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study explored how in-context learning (ICL) in large language models can be disrupted by data poisoning attacks in the setting of public health sentiment analysis. Using tweets of Human Metapneumovirus (HMPV), small adversarial perturbations such as synonym replacement, negation insertion, and randomized perturbation were introduced into the support examples. Even these minor manipulations caused major disruptions, with sentiment labels flipping in up to 67% of cases. To address this, a Spectral Signature Defense was applied, which filtered out poisoned examples while keeping the data's meaning and sentiment intact. After defense, ICL accuracy remained steady at around 46.7%, and logistic regression validation reached 100% accuracy, showing that the defense successfully preserved the dataset's integrity. Overall, the findings extend prior theoretical studies of ICL poisoning to a practical, high-stakes setting in public health discourse analysis, highlighting both the risks and potential defenses for robust LLM deployment. This study also highlights the fragility of ICL under attack and the value of spectral defenses in making AI systems more reliable for health-related social media monitoring."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03636","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:54:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03633","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03633","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03633","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Vuong","name":"Vuong, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan","familyName":"Gauch","name":"Gauch, Susan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Predicting Stock Price Movement with LLM-Enhanced Tweet Emotion Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T02:31:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:19:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurately predicting short-term stock price movement remains a challenging task due to the market's inherent volatility and sensitivity to investor sentiment. This paper discusses a deep learning framework that integrates emotion features extracted from tweet data with historical stock price information to forecast significant price changes on the following day. We utilize Meta's Llama 3.1-8B-Instruct model to preprocess tweet data, thereby enhancing the quality of emotion features derived from three emotion analysis approaches: a transformer-based DistilRoBERTa classifier from the Hugging Face library and two lexicon-based methods using National Research Council Canada (NRC) resources. These features are combined with previous-day stock price data to train a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model. Experimental results on TSLA, AAPL, and AMZN stocks show that all three emotion analysis methods improve the average accuracy for predicting significant price movements, compared to the baseline model using only historical stock prices, which yields an accuracy of 13.5%. The DistilRoBERTa-based stock prediction model achieves the best performance, with accuracy rising from 23.6% to 38.5% when using LLaMA-enhanced emotion analysis. These results demonstrate that using large language models to preprocess tweet content enhances the effectiveness of emotion analysis which in turn improves the accuracy of predicting significant stock price movements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17th International Conference on Knowledge Discovery, Knowledge Engineering and Knowledge Management (KDIR 2025), Marbella, Spain, Oct. 22-24, 2025 (to appear) Best Student Paper Finalist"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03633","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:54:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03612","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03612","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03612","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanqiu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Tanqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Pappas","name":"Pappas, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjun","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandesh","familyName":"Swamy","name":"Swamy, Sandesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Modal Content Optimization for Steering Web Agent Preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T01:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:18:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language model (VLM)-based web agents increasingly power high-stakes selection tasks like content recommendation or product ranking by combining multimodal perception with preference reasoning. Recent studies reveal that these agents are vulnerable against attackers who can bias selection outcomes through preference manipulations using adversarial pop-ups, image perturbations, or content tweaks. Existing work, however, either assumes strong white-box access, with limited single-modal perturbations, or uses impractical settings. In this paper, we demonstrate, for the first time, that joint exploitation of visual and textual channels yields significantly more powerful preference manipulations under realistic attacker capabilities. We introduce Cross-Modal Preference Steering (CPS) that jointly optimizes imperceptible modifications to an item's visual and natural language descriptions, exploiting CLIP-transferable image perturbations and RLHF-induced linguistic biases to steer agent decisions. In contrast to prior studies that assume gradient access, or control over webpages, or agent memory, we adopt a realistic black-box threat setup: a non-privileged adversary can edit only their own listing's images and textual metadata, with no insight into the agent's model internals. We evaluate CPS on agents powered by state-of-the-art proprietary and open source VLMs including GPT-4.1, Qwen-2.5VL and Pixtral-Large on both movie selection and e-commerce tasks. Our results show that CPS is significantly more effective than leading baseline methods. For instance, our results show that CPS consistently outperforms baselines across all models while maintaining 70% lower detection rates, demonstrating both effectiveness and stealth. These findings highlight an urgent need for robust defenses as agentic systems play an increasingly consequential role in society."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03612","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:54:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03611","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03611","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03611","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raquib Bin","familyName":"Yousuf","name":"Yousuf, Raquib Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadyant","familyName":"Khatri","name":"Khatri, Aadyant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengzhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shengzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mandar","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Mandar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naren","familyName":"Ramakrishnan","name":"Ramakrishnan, Naren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can an LLM Induce a Graph? Investigating Memory Drift and Context Length"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T01:56:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:17:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently proposed evaluation benchmarks aim to characterize the effective context length and the forgetting tendencies of large language models (LLMs). However, these benchmarks often rely on simplistic 'needle in a haystack' retrieval or continuation tasks that may not accurately reflect the performance of these models in information-dense scenarios. Thus, rather than simple next token prediction, we argue for evaluating these models on more complex reasoning tasks that requires them to induce structured relational knowledge from the text - such as graphs from potentially noisy natural language content. While the input text can be viewed as generated in terms of a graph, its structure is not made explicit and connections must be induced from distributed textual cues, separated by long contexts and interspersed with irrelevant information. Our findings reveal that LLMs begin to exhibit memory drift and contextual forgetting at much shorter effective lengths when tasked with this form of relational reasoning, compared to what existing benchmarks suggest. With these findings, we offer recommendations for the optimal use of popular LLMs for complex reasoning tasks. We further show that even models specialized for reasoning, such as OpenAI o1, remain vulnerable to early memory drift in these settings. These results point to significant limitations in the models' ability to abstract structured knowledge from unstructured input and highlight the need for architectural adaptations to improve long-range reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2025 IEEE International Conference on Knowledge Graph (ICKG)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03611","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:54:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mattia","familyName":"Scardecchia","name":"Scardecchia, Mattia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unsupervised Transformer Pre-Training for Images: Self-Distillation, Mean Teachers, and Random Crops"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T01:38:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:17:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in self-supervised learning (SSL) have made it possible to learn general-purpose visual features that capture both the high-level semantics and the fine-grained spatial structure of images. Most notably, the recent DINOv2 has established a new state of the art by surpassing weakly supervised methods (WSL) like OpenCLIP on most benchmarks. In this survey, we examine the core ideas behind its approach, multi-crop view augmentation and self-distillation with a mean teacher, and trace their development in previous work. We then compare the performance of DINO and DINOv2 with other SSL and WSL methods across various downstream tasks, and highlight some remarkable emergent properties of their learned features with transformer backbones. We conclude by briefly discussing DINOv2's limitations, its impact, and future research directions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:54:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:54:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:54:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander V.","familyName":"Mantzaris","name":"Mantzaris, Alexander V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the Hierarchical Reasoning Model for Small Natural-Image Classification Without Augmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T01:22:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:17:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper asks whether the Hierarchical Reasoning Model (HRM) with the two Transformer-style modules $(f_L,f_H)$, one step (DEQ-style) training, deep supervision, Rotary Position Embeddings, and RMSNorm can serve as a practical image classifier. It is evaluated on MNIST, CIFAR-10, and CIFAR-100 under a deliberately raw regime: no data augmentation, identical optimizer family with one-epoch warmup then cosine-floor decay, and label smoothing. HRM optimizes stably and performs well on MNIST ($\\approx 98\\%$ test accuracy), but on small natural images it overfits and generalizes poorly: on CIFAR-10, HRM reaches 65.0\\% after 25 epochs, whereas a two-stage Conv--BN--ReLU baseline attains 77.2\\% while training $\\sim 30\\times$ faster per epoch; on CIFAR-100, HRM achieves only 29.7\\% test accuracy despite 91.5\\% train accuracy, while the same CNN reaches 45.3\\% test with 50.5\\% train accuracy. Loss traces and error analyses indicate healthy optimization but insufficient image-specific inductive bias for HRM in this regime. It is concluded that, for small-resolution image classification without augmentation, HRM is not competitive with even simple convolutional architectures as the HRM currently exist but this does not exclude possibilities that modifications to the model may allow it to improve greatly."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:53:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anvith","familyName":"Pabba","name":"Pabba, Anvith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Simin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Mathai","name":"Mathai, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anindya","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Anindya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baishakhi","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Baishakhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REFINE: Enhancing Program Repair Agents through Context-Aware Patch Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T00:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:16:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently shown strong potential in automatic program repair (APR), especially in repository-level settings where the goal is to generate patches based on natural language issue descriptions, large codebases, and regression tests. However, despite their promise, current LLM-based APR techniques often struggle to produce correct fixes due to limited understanding of code context and over-reliance on incomplete test suites. As a result, they frequently generate Draft Patches-partially correct patches that either incompletely address the bug or overfit to the test cases. In this work, we propose a novel patch refinement framework, Refine, that systematically transforms Draft Patches into correct ones. Refine addresses three key challenges: disambiguating vague issue and code context, diversifying patch candidates through test-time scaling, and aggregating partial fixes via an LLM-powered code review process. We implement Refine as a general refinement module that can be integrated into both open-agent-based and workflow-based APR systems. Our evaluation on the SWE-Bench Lite benchmark shows that Refine achieves state-of-the-art results among workflow-based approaches and approaches the best-known performance across all APR categories. Specifically, Refine boosts AutoCodeRover's performance by 14.67%, achieving a score of 51.67% and surpassing all prior baselines. On SWE-Bench Verified, Refine improves the resolution rate by 12.2%, and when integrated across multiple APR systems, it yields an average improvement of 14%-demonstrating its broad effectiveness and generalizability. These results highlight the effectiveness of refinement as a missing component in current APR pipelines and the potential of agentic collaboration in closing the gap between near-correct and correct patches. We also open source our code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"We also open source our code at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/SemAgent-7B2F/README.md"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:53:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikram","familyName":"Belmadani","name":"Belmadani, Ikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parisa Nazari","familyName":"Hashemi","name":"Hashemi, Parisa Nazari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Sebbag","name":"Sebbag, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Favre","name":"Favre, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Fortier","name":"Fortier, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Solen","familyName":"Quiniou","name":"Quiniou, Solen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Morin","name":"Morin, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Dufour","name":"Dufour, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM, Reporting In! Medical Information Extraction Across Prompting, Fine-tuning and Post-correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:59:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:15:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work presents our participation in the EvalLLM 2025 challenge on biomedical Named Entity Recognition (NER) and health event extraction in French (few-shot setting). For NER, we propose three approaches combining large language models (LLMs), annotation guidelines, synthetic data, and post-processing: (1) in-context learning (ICL) with GPT-4.1, incorporating automatic selection of 10 examples and a summary of the annotation guidelines into the prompt, (2) the universal NER system GLiNER, fine-tuned on a synthetic corpus and then verified by an LLM in post-processing, and (3) the open LLM LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, fine-tuned on the same synthetic corpus. Event extraction uses the same ICL strategy with GPT-4.1, reusing the guideline summary in the prompt. Results show GPT-4.1 leads with a macro-F1 of 61.53% for NER and 15.02% for event extraction, highlighting the importance of well-crafted prompting to maximize performance in very low-resource scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"in French language"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:53:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwin","familyName":"Prabu","name":"Prabu, Ashwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhat Thanh","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Nhat Thanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guofa","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guofa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CrossLag: Predicting Major Dengue Outbreaks with a Domain Knowledge Informed Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:30:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A variety of models have been developed to forecast dengue cases to date. However, it remains a challenge to predict major dengue outbreaks that need timely public warnings the most. In this paper, we introduce CrossLag, an environmentally informed attention that allows for the incorporation of lagging endogenous signals behind the significant events in the exogenous data into the architecture of the transformer at low parameter counts. Outbreaks typically lag behind major changes in climate and oceanic anomalies. We use TimeXer, a recent general-purpose transformer distinguishing exogenous-endogenous inputs, as the baseline for this study. Our proposed model outperforms TimeXer by a considerable margin in detecting and predicting major outbreaks in Singapore dengue data over a 24-week prediction window."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"(C) 2025 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:53:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:53:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:53:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Filipek","name":"Filipek, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reactive Transformer (RxT) -- Stateful Real-Time Processing for Event-Driven Reactive Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:18:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Transformer architecture has become the de facto standard for Large Language Models (LLMs), demonstrating remarkable capabilities in language understanding and generation. However, its application in conversational AI is fundamentally constrained by its stateless nature and the quadratic computational complexity ($O(L^2)$) with respect to sequence length $L$. Current models emulate memory by reprocessing an ever-expanding conversation history with each turn, leading to prohibitive costs and latency in long dialogues. This paper introduces the Reactive Transformer (RxT), a novel architecture designed to overcome these limitations by shifting from a data-driven to an event-driven paradigm. RxT processes each conversational turn as a discrete event in real-time, maintaining context in an integrated, fixed-size Short-Term Memory (STM) system. The architecture features a distinct operational cycle where a generator-decoder produces a response based on the current query and the previous memory state, after which a memory-encoder and a dedicated Memory Attention network asynchronously update the STM with a representation of the complete interaction. This design fundamentally alters the scaling dynamics, reducing the total user-facing cost of a conversation from quadratic ($O(N^2 \\cdot T)$) to linear ($O(N \\cdot T)$) with respect to the number of interactions $N$. By decoupling response generation from memory updates, RxT achieves low latency, enabling truly real-time, stateful, and economically viable long-form conversations. We validated our architecture with a series of proof-of-concept experiments on synthetic data, demonstrating superior performance and constant-time inference latency compared to a baseline stateless model of comparable size."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:52:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shen","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renran","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Renran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicole","familyName":"Adams","name":"Adams, Nicole","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Real-Time Assessment of Bystander Situation Awareness in Drone-Assisted First Aid"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T23:11:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Rapid naloxone delivery via drones offers a promising solution for responding to opioid overdose emergencies (OOEs), by extending lifesaving interventions to medically untrained bystanders before emergency medical services (EMS) arrive. Recognizing the critical role of bystander situational awareness (SA) in human-autonomy teaming (HAT), we address a key research gap in real-time SA assessment by introducing the Drone-Assisted Naloxone Delivery Simulation Dataset (DANDSD). This pioneering dataset captures HAT during simulated OOEs, where college students without medical training act as bystanders tasked with administering intranasal naloxone to a mock overdose victim. Leveraging this dataset, we propose a video-based real-time SA assessment framework that utilizes graph embeddings and transformer models to assess bystander SA in real time. Our approach integrates visual perception and comprehension cues--such as geometric, kinematic, and interaction graph features--and achieves high-performance SA prediction. It also demonstrates strong temporal segmentation accuracy, outperforming the FINCH baseline by 9% in Mean over Frames (MoF) and 5% in Intersection over Union (IoU). This work supports the development of adaptive drone systems capable of guiding bystanders effectively, ultimately improving emergency response outcomes and saving lives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:52:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03543","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03543","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03543","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evandros","familyName":"Kaklamanos","name":"Kaklamanos, Evandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristjana","familyName":"Kristinsdottir","name":"Kristinsdottir, Kristjana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dustin","familyName":"Carlson","name":"Carlson, Dustin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajesh","familyName":"Keswani","name":"Keswani, Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Pandolfino","name":"Pandolfino, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mozziyar","familyName":"Etemadi","name":"Etemadi, Mozziyar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Scope to Script: An Automated Report Generation Model for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:25:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:13:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Endoscopic procedures such as esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) and colonoscopy play a critical role in diagnosing and managing gastrointestinal (GI) disorders. However, the documentation burden associated with these procedures place significant strain on gastroenterologists, contributing to inefficiencies in clinical workflows and physician burnout. To address this challenge, we propose a novel automated report generation model that leverages a transformer-based vision encoder and text decoder within a two-stage training framework. In the first stage, both components are pre-trained on image/text caption pairs to capture generalized vision-language features, followed by fine-tuning on images/report pairs to generate clinically meaningful findings. Our approach not only streamlines the documentation process but also holds promise for reducing physician workload and improving patient care."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03543","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:52:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03541","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03541","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03541","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Halterman","name":"Halterman, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katherine A.","familyName":"Keith","name":"Keith, Katherine A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What is a protest anyway? Codebook conceptualization is still a first-order concern in LLM-era classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T22:19:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:13:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative large language models (LLMs) are now used extensively for text classification in computational social science (CSS). In this work, focus on the steps before and after LLM prompting -- conceptualization of concepts to be classified and using LLM predictions in downstream statistical inference -- which we argue have been overlooked in much of LLM-era CSS. We claim LLMs can tempt analysts to skip the conceptualization step, creating conceptualization errors that bias downstream estimates. Using simulations, we show that this conceptualization-induced bias cannot be corrected for solely by increasing LLM accuracy or post-hoc bias correction methods. We conclude by reminding CSS analysts that conceptualization is still a first-order concern in the LLM-era and provide concrete advice on how to pursue low-cost, unbiased, low-variance downstream estimates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03541","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:52:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:52:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:52:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03521","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03521","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03521","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Elahi","name":"Elahi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Identifying Financial Risk Information Using RAG with a Contrastive Insight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T21:24:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:11:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In specialized domains, humans often compare new problems against similar examples, highlight nuances, and draw conclusions instead of analyzing information in isolation. When applying reasoning in specialized contexts with LLMs on top of a RAG, the pipeline can capture contextually relevant information, but it is not designed to retrieve comparable cases or related problems.\n While RAG is effective at extracting factual information, its outputs in specialized reasoning tasks often remain generic, reflecting broad facts rather than context-specific insights. In finance, it results in generic risks that are true for the majority of companies. To address this limitation, we propose a peer-aware comparative inference layer on top of RAG.\n Our contrastive approach outperforms baseline RAG in text generation metrics such as ROUGE and BERTScore in comparison with human-generated equity research and risk."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 1 figure, Workshop on Generative AI in Finance, NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03521","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:51:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianghuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Lianghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagnik","familyName":"Anupam","name":"Anupam, Sagnik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Insup","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Insup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Osbert","familyName":"Bastani","name":"Bastani, Osbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RAPID: An Efficient Reinforcement Learning Algorithm for Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:58:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:10:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a promising strategy for finetuning small language models (SLMs) to solve targeted tasks such as math and coding. However, RL algorithms tend to be resource-intensive, taking a significant amount of time to train. We propose RAPID, a novel RL algorithm that can substantially reduce the running time of RL. Our key insight is that RL tends to be costly due to the need to perform both inference and backpropagation during training. To maximize use of computational resources, our algorithm performs inference in large batches, and then performs off-policy policy gradient updates in mini-batches. For off-policy updates, we incorporate group advantage estimation into the policy gradient algorithm, and derive an importance weighted estimator to correct for the bias arising from off-policy learning. Our experiments demonstrate that our algorithm can reduce running time by 11%-34% on three benchmarks compared to state-of-the-art RL algorithms while maintaining similar or better accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:51:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby","familyName":"Drinkall","name":"Drinkall, Toby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Red Lines and Grey Zones in the Fog of War: Benchmarking Legal Risk, Moral Harm, and Regional Bias in Large Language Model Military Decision-Making"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T20:55:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:10:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As military organisations consider integrating large language models (LLMs) into command and control (C2) systems for planning and decision support, understanding their behavioural tendencies is critical. This study develops a benchmarking framework for evaluating aspects of legal and moral risk in targeting behaviour by comparing LLMs acting as agents in multi-turn simulated conflict. We introduce four metrics grounded in International Humanitarian Law (IHL) and military doctrine: Civilian Target Rate (CTR) and Dual-use Target Rate (DTR) assess compliance with legal targeting principles, while Mean and Max Simulated Non-combatant Casualty Value (SNCV) quantify tolerance for civilian harm.\n We evaluate three frontier models, GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5, and LLaMA-3.1, through 90 multi-agent, multi-turn crisis simulations across three geographic regions. Our findings reveal that off-the-shelf LLMs exhibit concerning and unpredictable targeting behaviour in simulated conflict environments. All models violated the IHL principle of distinction by targeting civilian objects, with breach rates ranging from 16.7% to 66.7%. Harm tolerance escalated through crisis simulations with MeanSNCV increasing from 16.5 in early turns to 27.7 in late turns. Significant inter-model variation emerged: LLaMA-3.1 selected an average of 3.47 civilian strikes per simulation with MeanSNCV of 28.4, while Gemini-2.5 selected 0.90 civilian strikes with MeanSNCV of 17.6. These differences indicate that model selection for deployment constitutes a choice about acceptable legal and moral risk profiles in military operations.\n This work seeks to provide a proof-of-concept of potential behavioural risks that could emerge from the use of LLMs in Decision Support Systems (AI DSS) as well as a reproducible benchmarking framework with interpretable metrics for standardising pre-deployment testing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"54 pages; 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:51:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:51:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:51:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mengyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenfei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yauhen","familyName":"Babakhin","name":"Babakhin, Yauhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Moreira","name":"Moreira, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronay","familyName":"Ak","name":"Ak, Ronay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radek","familyName":"Osmulski","name":"Osmulski, Radek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Even","familyName":"Oldridge","name":"Oldridge, Even","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benedikt","familyName":"Schifferer","name":"Schifferer, Benedikt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Omni-Embed-Nemotron: A Unified Multimodal Retrieval Model for Text, Image, Audio, and Video"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:07:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Omni-Embed-Nemotron, a unified multimodal retrieval embedding model developed to handle the increasing complexity of real-world information needs. While Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has significantly advanced language models by incorporating external knowledge, existing text-based retrievers rely on clean, structured input and struggle with the visually and semantically rich content found in real-world documents such as PDFs, slides, or videos. Recent work such as ColPali has shown that preserving document layout using image-based representations can improve retrieval quality. Building on this, and inspired by the capabilities of recent multimodal models such as Qwen2.5-Omni, we extend retrieval beyond text and images to also support audio and video modalities. Omni-Embed-Nemotron enables both cross-modal (e.g., text - video) and joint-modal (e.g., text - video+audio) retrieval using a single model. We describe the architecture, training setup, and evaluation results of Omni-Embed-Nemotron, and demonstrate its effectiveness in text, image, and video retrieval."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03455","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03455","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03455","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sejuti","familyName":"Majumder","name":"Majumder, Sejuti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saarthak","familyName":"Kapse","name":"Kapse, Saarthak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moinak","familyName":"Bhattacharya","name":"Bhattacharya, Moinak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alisa","familyName":"Yurovsky","name":"Yurovsky, Alisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prateek","familyName":"Prasanna","name":"Prasanna, Prateek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PEaRL: Pathway-Enhanced Representation Learning for Gene and Pathway Expression Prediction from Histology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:21:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:07:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating histopathology with spatial transcriptomics (ST) provides a powerful opportunity to link tissue morphology with molecular function. Yet most existing multimodal approaches rely on a small set of highly variable genes, which limits predictive scope and overlooks the coordinated biological programs that shape tissue phenotypes. We present PEaRL (Pathway Enhanced Representation Learning), a multimodal framework that represents transcriptomics through pathway activation scores computed with ssGSEA. By encoding biologically coherent pathway signals with a transformer and aligning them with histology features via contrastive learning, PEaRL reduces dimensionality, improves interpretability, and strengthens cross-modal correspondence. Across three cancer ST datasets (breast, skin, and lymph node), PEaRL consistently outperforms SOTA methods, yielding higher accuracy for both gene- and pathway-level expression prediction (up to 58.9 percent and 20.4 percent increase in Pearson correlation coefficient compared to SOTA). These results demonstrate that grounding transcriptomic representation in pathways produces more biologically faithful and interpretable multimodal models, advancing computational pathology beyond gene-level embeddings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03455","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03442","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03442","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03442","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ege","familyName":"Cakar","name":"Cakar, Ege","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Per Ola","familyName":"Kristensson","name":"Kristensson, Per Ola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Argument is the Explanation: Structured Argumentation for Trust in Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Humans are black boxes -- we cannot observe their neural processes, yet society functions by evaluating verifiable arguments. AI explainability should follow this principle: stakeholders need verifiable reasoning chains, not mechanistic transparency. We propose using structured argumentation to provide a level of explanation and verification neither interpretability nor LLM-generated explanation is able to offer. Our pipeline achieves state-of-the-art 94.44 macro F1 on the AAEC published train/test split (5.7 points above prior work) and $0.81$ macro F1, $\\sim$0.07 above previous published results with comparable data setups, for Argumentative MicroTexts relation classification, converting LLM text into argument graphs and enabling verification at each inferential step. We demonstrate this idea on multi-agent risk assessment using the Structured What-If Technique, where specialized agents collaborate transparently to carry out risk assessment otherwise achieved by humans alone. Using Bipolar Assumption-Based Argumentation, we capture support/attack relationships, thereby enabling automatic hallucination detection via fact nodes attacking arguments. We also provide a verification mechanism that enables iterative refinement through test-time feedback without retraining. For easy deployment, we provide a Docker container for the fine-tuned AMT model, and the rest of the code with the Bipolar ABA Python package on GitHub."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures, 6 tables, submitted to IAAI-26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03442","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:50:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:50:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:50:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chashi Mahiul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Chashi Mahiul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oteo","familyName":"Mamo","name":"Mamo, Oteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel Jacob","familyName":"Chacko","name":"Chacko, Samuel Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiuwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weikuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatial-ViLT: Enhancing Visual Spatial Reasoning through Multi-Task Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T45, 68T10, 68T40","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T19:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) have advanced multimodal reasoning but still face challenges in spatial reasoning for 3D scenes and complex object configurations. To address this, we introduce SpatialViLT, an enhanced VLM that integrates spatial features like depth maps, 3D coordinates, and edge maps through a multi-task learning framework. This approach enriches multimodal embeddings with spatial understanding. We propose two variants: SpatialViLT and MaskedSpatialViLT, focusing on full and masked object regions, respectively. Additionally, SpatialEnsemble combines both approaches, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy. Our models excel in spatial reasoning categories such as directional, topological, and proximity relations, as demonstrated on the challenging Visual Spatial Reasoning (VSR) dataset. This work represents a significant step in enhancing the spatial intelligence of AI systems, crucial for advanced multimodal understanding and real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:49:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congzheng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Congzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-Efficient Backpropagation for Fine-Tuning LLMs on Resource-Constrained Mobile Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:36:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) with backpropagation\\textemdash even for a subset of parameters such as LoRA\\textemdash can be much more memory-consuming than inference and is often deemed impractical for resource-constrained mobile devices. Alternative methods, such as zeroth-order optimization (ZO), can greatly reduce the memory footprint but come at the cost of significantly slower model convergence (10$\\times$ to 100$\\times$ more steps than backpropagation). We propose a memory-efficient implementation of backpropagation (MeBP) on mobile devices that provides better trade-off between memory usage and compute time, while converging faster and achieving better performance than the ZO baseline. We verify the effectiveness of MeBP on an iPhone 15 Pro Max and show that various LLMs, ranging from 0.5B to 4B parameters, can be fine-tuned using less than 1GB of memory. We release an example of the MeBP implementation at https://github.com/apple/ml-mebp."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:49:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:49:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:49:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Shrivastava","name":"Shrivastava, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ari","familyName":"Holtzman","name":"Holtzman, Ari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Know Thyself? On the Incapability and Implications of AI Self-Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:00:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-recognition is a crucial metacognitive capability for AI systems, relevant not only for psychological analysis but also for safety, particularly in evaluative scenarios. Motivated by contradictory interpretations of whether models possess self-recognition (Panickssery et al., 2024; Davidson et al., 2024), we introduce a systematic evaluation framework that can be easily applied and updated. Specifically, we measure how well 10 contemporary larger language models (LLMs) can identify their own generated text versus text from other models through two tasks: binary self-recognition and exact model prediction. Different from prior claims, our results reveal a consistent failure in self-recognition. Only 4 out of 10 models predict themselves as generators, and the performance is rarely above random chance. Additionally, models exhibit a strong bias toward predicting GPT and Claude families. We also provide the first evaluation of model awareness of their own and others' existence, as well as the reasoning behind their choices in self-recognition. We find that the model demonstrates some knowledge of its own existence and other models, but their reasoning reveals a hierarchical bias. They appear to assume that GPT, Claude, and occasionally Gemini are the top-tier models, often associating high-quality text with them. We conclude by discussing the implications of our findings on AI safety and future directions to develop appropriate AI self-awareness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Our code is available, see https://github.com/ChicagoHAI/self-recognition"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03397","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03397","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03397","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Morales-Alvarado","name":"Morales-Alvarado, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Foundation models for equation discovery in high energy physics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T18:00:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:04:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models, large machine learning models trained on broad, multimodal datasets, have been gaining increasing attention in scientific applications due to their strong performance on diverse downstream tasks. Large Language Models (LLMs), a prominent instance of foundation models, have achieved remarkable success in tasks such as text and image generation. In this work, we investigate their potential for equation discovery in high energy physics, focusing on symbolic regression. We apply the LLM-SR methodology both to benchmark problems of equation recovery in lepton angular distributions and to the discovery of functional forms for angular coefficients in electroweak boson production at the Large Hadron Collider, observables of high phenomenological relevance for which no closed-form expressions are known from first principles. Our results demonstrate that LLM-SR can uncover compact, accurate, and interpretable equations across in-domain and out-of-domain kinematic regions, effectively incorporating embedded scientific knowledge and offering a promising new approach to equation discovery in high energy physics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03397","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03384","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03384","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03384","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Arunasalam","name":"Arunasalam, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madison","familyName":"Pickering","name":"Pickering, Madison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z. Berkay","familyName":"Celik","name":"Celik, Z. Berkay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Blase","familyName":"Ur","name":"Ur, Blase","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Implicit Values Embedded in How Humans and LLMs Complete Subjective Everyday Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T16:52:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) can underpin AI assistants that help users with everyday tasks, such as by making recommendations or performing basic computation. Despite AI assistants' promise, little is known about the implicit values these assistants display while completing subjective everyday tasks. Humans may consider values like environmentalism, charity, and diversity. To what extent do LLMs exhibit these values in completing everyday tasks? How do they compare with humans? We answer these questions by auditing how six popular LLMs complete 30 everyday tasks, comparing LLMs to each other and to 100 human crowdworkers from the US. We find LLMs often do not align with humans, nor with other LLMs, in the implicit values exhibited."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03384","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03376","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03376","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03376","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjukta","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Sanjukta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Language Model as a Judge for Object Detection in Industrial Diagrams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T13:52:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Industrial diagrams such as piping and instrumentation diagrams (P\u0026amp;IDs) are essential for the design, operation, and maintenance of industrial plants. Converting these diagrams into digital form is an important step toward building digital twins and enabling intelligent industrial automation. A central challenge in this digitalization process is accurate object detection. Although recent advances have significantly improved object detection algorithms, there remains a lack of methods to automatically evaluate the quality of their outputs. This paper addresses this gap by introducing a framework that employs Visual Language Models (VLMs) to assess object detection results and guide their refinement. The approach exploits the multimodal capabilities of VLMs to identify missing or inconsistent detections, thereby enabling automated quality assessment and improving overall detection performance on complex industrial diagrams."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Pre-review version submitted to IEEE ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03376","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasho","familyName":"Nedelkoski","name":"Nedelkoski, Sasho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Acker","name":"Acker, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Odej","familyName":"Kao","name":"Kao, Odej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soeren","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Soeren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Scheinert","name":"Scheinert, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Low-Communication Training with Decoupled Momentum Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The training of large models demands substantial computational resources, typically available only in data centers with high-bandwidth interconnects. However, reducing the reliance on high-bandwidth interconnects between nodes enables the use of distributed compute resources as an alternative to centralized data center training. Building on recent advances in distributed model training, we propose an approach that further reduces communication by combining infrequent synchronizations across distributed model replicas with gradient momentum compression. In particular, we treat the optimizer momentum as a signal and decompose the Nesterov momentum into high- and low-frequency components via the discrete cosine transform (DCT). Only the high-frequency components are synchronized across model replicas every $H$ steps. Empirically, our method achieves up to a $16\\times$ reduction in communication compared to the baseline DiLoCo, and it generalizes across architectures, including transformer-based language models and convolutional neural networks for images. Overall, this work advances the feasibility of training large models on distributed nodes with low-bandwidth interconnects."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 - DynaFront 2025: Dynamics at the Frontiers of Optimization, Sampling, and Games Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:48:17Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:48:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:48:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03358","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03358","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03358","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Annan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danielle C.","familyName":"Maddix","name":"Maddix, Danielle C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boran","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Boran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul Fatir","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Abdul Fatir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleksandr","familyName":"Shchur","name":"Shchur, Oleksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Faloutsos","name":"Faloutsos, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew Gordon","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Andrew Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Transformers for Time Series: Rank Structure, Flow-of-ranks, and Compressibility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T23:56:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers are widely used across data modalities, and yet the principles distilled from text models often transfer imperfectly to models trained to other modalities. In this paper, we analyze Transformers through the lens of rank structure. Our focus is on the time series setting, where the structural properties of the data differ remarkably from those of text or vision. We show that time-series embeddings, unlike text or vision, exhibit sharply decaying singular value spectra: small patch sizes and smooth continuous mappings concentrate the data into low-rank subspaces. From this, we prove that the associated $Q/K/V$ projections admit accurate low-rank approximations, and that attention layers become compressible in proportion to the decay of the embedding spectrum. We introduce the concept of flow-of-ranks, a phenomenon by which nonlinear mixing across depth inflates the rank, explaining why early layers are most amenable to compression and why ranks grow with depth. Guided by these theoretical and empirical results, we use these insights to compress Chronos, a large time series foundation model, achieving a reduction of $65\\%$ in inference time and $81\\%$ in memory, without loss of accuracy. Our findings provide principled guidance for allocating width, depth, and heads in time series foundation models, and for exploiting their inherent compressibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03358","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03351","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03351","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03351","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jundong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jundong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Rasero","name":"Rasero, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aiying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Aiying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirag","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Chirag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interpretable Neuropsychiatric Diagnosis via Concept-Guided Graph Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T19:38:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nearly one in five adolescents currently live with a diagnosed mental or behavioral health condition, such as anxiety, depression, or conduct disorder, underscoring the urgency of developing accurate and interpretable diagnostic tools. Resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rs-fMRI) provides a powerful lens into large-scale functional connectivity, where brain regions are modeled as nodes and inter-regional synchrony as edges, offering clinically relevant biomarkers for psychiatric disorders. While prior works use graph neural network (GNN) approaches for disorder prediction, they remain complex black-boxes, limiting their reliability and clinical translation. In this work, we propose CONCEPTNEURO, a concept-based diagnosis framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) and neurobiological domain knowledge to automatically generate, filter, and encode interpretable functional connectivity concepts. Each concept is represented as a structured subgraph linking specific brain regions, which are then passed through a concept classifier. Our design ensures predictions through clinically meaningful connectivity patterns, enabling both interpretability and strong predictive performance. Extensive experiments across multiple psychiatric disorder datasets demonstrate that CONCEPTNEURO-augmented GNNs consistently outperform their vanilla counterparts, improving accuracy while providing transparent, clinically aligned explanations. Furthermore, concept analyses highlight disorder-specific connectivity patterns that align with expert knowledge and suggest new hypotheses for future investigation, establishing CONCEPTNEURO as an interpretable, domain-informed framework for psychiatric disorder diagnosis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03351","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03349","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03349","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03349","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentCaster: Reasoning-Guided Tornado Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Atmospheric and Oceanic Physics (physics.ao-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:57:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There is a growing need to evaluate Large Language Models (LLMs) on complex, high-impact, real-world tasks to assess their true readiness as reasoning agents. To address this gap, we introduce AgentCaster, a contamination-free framework employing multimodal LLMs end-to-end for the challenging, long-horizon task of tornado forecasting. Within AgentCaster, models interpret heterogeneous spatiotemporal data from a high-resolution convection-allowing forecast archive. We assess model performance over a 40-day period featuring diverse historical data, spanning several major tornado outbreaks and including over 500 tornado reports. Each day, models query interactively from a pool of 3,625 forecast maps and 40,125 forecast soundings for a forecast horizon of 12-36 hours. Probabilistic tornado-risk polygon predictions are verified against ground truths derived from geometric comparisons across disjoint risk bands in projected coordinate space. To quantify accuracy, we propose domain-specific TornadoBench and TornadoHallucination metrics, with TornadoBench highly challenging for both LLMs and domain expert human forecasters. Notably, human experts significantly outperform state-of-the-art models, which demonstrate a strong tendency to hallucinate and overpredict risk intensity, struggle with precise geographic placement, and exhibit poor spatiotemporal reasoning in complex, dynamically evolving systems. AgentCaster aims to advance research on improving LLM agents for challenging reasoning tasks in critical domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03349","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03341","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03341","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03341","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bozheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhenhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiawang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mushui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mushui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongliang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangguang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Yangguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Licheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpusAnimation: Code-Based Dynamic Chart Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T13:19:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dynamic Chart Generation (DCG) involves producing code-rendered animated visualizations as charts. While recent advances in multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) have significantly improved their capability on static chart generation and comprehension, MLLMs' potential for handling dynamic chart generation and understanding remains underexplored. To bridge this research gap, we introduce DCG-Bench (Dynamic Chart Generation Benchmark), the first benchmark evaluating MLLM's capability on dynamic chart generation tasks from three dimensions: Simple Text-to-Chart, Detailed Text-to-Chart, and Video-to-Chart tasks. We construct DCG-8K, a high-quality DCG dataset with annotations covering instruction-code-video triplets and QA pairs for both code and video evaluation. Based on DCG-8K, we explored a two-stage training recipe, proposing Joint-Code-Visual Reward for group relative policy optimization to construct expert MLLM Qwen2.5-VL-DCG-3B for the DCG task. Our benchmarking result reveals shortcomings of existing MLLMs in the visual-to-chart task, and our model beats the best open-sourced MLLM with an average 8.31% performance gain across three tasks, and shows on par performance against proprietary models with only 3B parameters, proving the effectiveness of our training recipe. Our code and dataset will be publicly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"working in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03341","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofiane","familyName":"Ennadir","name":"Ennadir, Sofiane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Levente","familyName":"Zólyomi","name":"Zólyomi, Levente","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oleg","familyName":"Smirnov","name":"Smirnov, Oleg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Pertoft","name":"Pertoft, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filip","familyName":"Cornell","name":"Cornell, Filip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lele","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Lele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pool Me Wisely: On the Effect of Pooling in Transformer-Based Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T11:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer models have become the dominant backbone for sequence modeling, leveraging self-attention to produce contextualized token representations. These are typically aggregated into fixed-size vectors via pooling operations for downstream tasks. While much of the literature has focused on attention mechanisms, the role of pooling remains underexplored despite its critical impact on model behavior. In this paper, we introduce a theoretical framework that rigorously characterizes the expressivity of Transformer-based models equipped with widely used pooling methods by deriving closed-form bounds on their representational capacity and the ability to distinguish similar inputs. Our analysis extends to different variations of attention formulations, demonstrating that these bounds hold across diverse architectural variants. We empirically evaluate pooling strategies across tasks requiring both global and local contextual understanding, spanning three major modalities: computer vision, natural language processing, and time-series analysis. Results reveal consistent trends in how pooling choices affect accuracy, sensitivity, and optimization behavior. Our findings unify theoretical and empirical perspectives, providing practical guidance for selecting or designing pooling mechanisms suited to specific tasks. This work positions pooling as a key architectural component in Transformer models and lays the foundation for more principled model design beyond attention alone."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zerui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zerui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana","familyName":"Klimovic","name":"Klimovic, Ana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonggang","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yonggang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic-Aware Scheduling for GPU Clusters with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T02:01:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning (DL) schedulers are pivotal in optimizing resource allocation in GPU clusters, but operate with a critical limitation: they are largely blind to the semantic context of the jobs they manage. This forces them to rely on limited metadata, leading to high profiling overhead, unreliable duration estimation, inadequate failure handling, and poor observability. To this end, we propose SchedMate, a framework that bridges this semantic gap by systematically extracting deep insights from overlooked, unstructured data sources: source code, runtime logs, and historical jobs. SchedMate enhances existing schedulers non-intrusively through three LLM-based components. Our implementation integrates seamlessly with existing deep learning schedulers. Evaluations on a 128-GPU physical cluster and extensive simulations on production traces show SchedMate reduces average job completion times by up to 1.91x, substantially enhancing the scheduling performance, demonstrating the critical role of semantic-awareness in modern DL scheduling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03331","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03331","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03331","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek","familyName":"Acharya","name":"Acharya, Vivek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Intelligent Healthcare Ecosystems: Optimizing the Iron Triangle of Healthcare (Access, Cost, Quality)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.1; J.3; H.3.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 92C55, 92C60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T20:10:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The United States spends nearly 17% of GDP on healthcare yet continues to face uneven access and outcomes. This well-known trade-off among cost, quality, and access - the \"iron triangle\" - motivates a system-level redesign. This paper proposes an Intelligent Healthcare Ecosystem (iHE): an integrated, data-driven framework that uses generative AI and large language models, federated learning, interoperability standards (FHIR, TEFCA), and digital twins to improve access and quality while lowering cost. We review historical spending trends, waste, and international comparisons; introduce a value equation that jointly optimizes access, quality, and cost; and synthesize evidence on the enabling technologies and operating model for iHE. Methods follow a narrative review of recent literature and policy reports. Results outline core components (AI decision support, interoperability, telehealth, automation) and show how iHE can reduce waste, personalize care, and support value-based payment while addressing privacy, bias, and adoption challenges. We argue that a coordinated iHE can bend - if not break - the iron triangle, moving the system toward care that is more accessible, affordable, and high quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 figures, formatted per MDPI guidelines, APA-style numbered references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03331","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:47:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:47:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:47:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03315","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03315","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03315","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Gibson","name":"Gibson, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decomposing Attention To Find Context-Sensitive Neurons"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:29:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study transformer language models, analyzing attention heads whose attention patterns are spread out, and whose attention scores depend weakly on content. We argue that the softmax denominators of these heads are stable when the underlying token distribution is fixed. By sampling softmax denominators from a \"calibration text\", we can combine together the outputs of multiple such stable heads in the first layer of GPT2-Small, approximating their combined output by a linear summary of the surrounding text. This approximation enables a procedure where from the weights alone - and a single calibration text - we can uncover hundreds of first layer neurons that respond to high-level contextual properties of the surrounding text, including neurons that didn't activate on the calibration text."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 7 figures. Submitted to the Mechanistic Interpretability Workshop at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03315","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Orr","name":"Orr, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Drew","familyName":"Cranford","name":"Cranford, Drew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Ford","name":"Ford, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Gluck","name":"Gluck, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Will","familyName":"Hancock","name":"Hancock, Will","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Lebiere","name":"Lebiere, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pete","familyName":"Pirolli","name":"Pirolli, Pete","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Ritter","name":"Ritter, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Stocco","name":"Stocco, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Not Even Wrong: On the Limits of Prediction as Explanation in Cognitive Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:23:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:02:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We offer a comment on the Centaur (Binz et al., 2025) transformer-based model of human behavior. In particular, Centaur was cast as a path towards unified theories of cognition. We offer a counter claim with supporting argument: Centaur is a path divergent from unified theories of cognition, one that moves towards a unified model of behavior sans cognition."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshar","familyName":"Gothi","name":"Gothi, Akshar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Convolutional Neural Nets vs Vision Transformers: A SpaceNet Case Study with Balanced vs Imbalanced Regimes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T21:41:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a controlled comparison of a convolutional neural network (EfficientNet-B0) and a Vision Transformer (ViT-Base) on SpaceNet under two label-distribution regimes: a naturally imbalanced five-class split and a balanced-resampled split with 700 images per class (70:20:10 train/val/test). With matched preprocessing (224x224, ImageNet normalization), lightweight augmentations, and a 40-epoch budget on a single NVIDIA P100, we report accuracy, macro-F1, balanced accuracy, per-class recall, and deployment metrics (model size and latency). On the imbalanced split, EfficientNet-B0 reaches 93% test accuracy with strong macro-F1 and lower latency; ViT-Base is competitive at 93% with a larger parameter count and runtime. On the balanced split, both models are strong; EfficientNet-B0 reaches 99% while ViT-Base remains competitive, indicating that balancing narrows architecture gaps while CNNs retain an efficiency edge. We release manifests, logs, and per-image predictions to support reproducibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, 9 tables. Code and artifacts: https://github.com/akshar27/spacenet-cnn-vs-vit (release v1.0.1)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Passant","familyName":"Elchafei","name":"Elchafei, Passant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amany","familyName":"Fashwan","name":"Fashwan, Amany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal Arabic Captioning with Interpretable Visual Concept Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T18:52:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present VLCAP, an Arabic image captioning framework that integrates CLIP-based visual label retrieval with multimodal text generation. Rather than relying solely on end-to-end captioning, VLCAP grounds generation in interpretable Arabic visual concepts extracted with three multilingual encoders, mCLIP, AraCLIP, and Jina V4, each evaluated separately for label retrieval. A hybrid vocabulary is built from training captions and enriched with about 21K general domain labels translated from the Visual Genome dataset, covering objects, attributes, and scenes. The top-k retrieved labels are transformed into fluent Arabic prompts and passed along with the original image to vision-language models. In the second stage, we tested Qwen-VL and Gemini Pro Vision for caption generation, resulting in six encoder-decoder configurations. The results show that mCLIP + Gemini Pro Vision achieved the best BLEU-1 (5.34%) and cosine similarity (60.01%), while AraCLIP + Qwen-VL obtained the highest LLM-judge score (36.33%). This interpretable pipeline enables culturally coherent and contextually accurate Arabic captions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03294","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03294","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03294","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saanvi","familyName":"Kataria","name":"Kataria, Saanvi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Domain-Robust Marine Plastic Detection Using Vision Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T17:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Marine plastic pollution is a pressing environmental threat, making reliable automation for underwater debris detection essential. However, vision systems trained on one dataset often degrade on new imagery due to domain shift. This study benchmarks models for cross-domain robustness, training convolutional neural networks - CNNs (MobileNetV2, ResNet-18, EfficientNet-B0) and vision transformers (DeiT-Tiny, ViT-B16) on a labeled underwater dataset and then evaluates them on a balanced cross-domain test set built from plastic-positive images drawn from a different source and negatives from the training domain. Two zero-shot models were assessed, CLIP ViT-L14 and Google's Gemini 2.0 Flash, that leverage pretraining to classify images without fine-tuning. Results show the lightweight MobileNetV2 delivers the strongest cross-domain performance (F1 0.97), surpassing larger models. All fine-tuned models achieved high Precision (around 99%), but differ in Recall, indicating varying sensitivity to plastic instances. Zero-shot CLIP is comparatively sensitive (Recall around 80%) yet prone to false positives (Precision around 56%), whereas Gemini exhibits the inverse profile (Precision around 99%, Recall around 81%). Error analysis highlights recurring confusions with coral textures, suspended particulates, and specular glare. Overall, compact CNNs with supervised training can generalize effectively for cross-domain underwater detection, while large pretrained vision-language models provide complementary strengths."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 5 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03294","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:26Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rana","familyName":"Shahout","name":"Shahout, Rana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Colin","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Colin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minlan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Minlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Mitzenmacher","name":"Mitzenmacher, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Score Distributions to Balance: Plug-and-Play Mixture-of-Experts Routing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T16:29:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models can scale parameter capacity by routing each token to a subset of experts through a learned gate function. While conditional routing reduces training costs, it shifts the burden on inference memory: expert parameters and activations consume memory, limiting the number of experts per device. As tokens are routed, some experts become overloaded while others are underutilized. Because experts are mapped to GPUs, this imbalance translates directly into degraded system performance in terms of latency, throughput, and cost. We present LASER, a plug-and-play, inference-time routing algorithm that balances load while preserving accuracy. LASER adapts to the shape of the gate's score distribution. When scores provide a clear preference, it routes to the strongest experts; when scores are more uniform, it broadens the set of viable experts and routes to the least-loaded among them. Because LASER relies only on gate scores from a trained model, it integrates directly into existing MoE inference pipelines without retraining or finetuning. We evaluate LASER on Mixtral-8x7B and DeepSeek-MoE-16b-chat across four datasets (ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, MMLU, and GSM8K). LASER improves load balancing, translating into lower latency and higher throughput, while keeping the accuracy changes negligible."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Su","familyName":"Kara","name":"Kara, Su","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fazle","familyName":"Faisal","name":"Faisal, Fazle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Nath","name":"Nath, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WAREX: Web Agent Reliability Evaluation on Existing Benchmarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T20:51:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in browser-based LLM agents have shown promise for automating tasks ranging from simple form filling to hotel booking or online shopping. Current benchmarks measure agent performance in controlled environments, such as containers or stable networks, where websites behave deterministically. However, in the real world, users access websites over networks and HTTPS connections that introduce instability from multiple sources: client-side, server-side issues or broader system failures. Moreover, live websites are prone to web attacks such Cross-Site Scripting, as well as general site modifications which can cause unexpected or malicious pop-ups or improper functionality. To address this gap, we present WAREX: Web Agent Reliability Evaluation on Existing Benchmarks. We measure the impact of WAREX across three popular benchmarks: WebArena, WebVoyager, and REAL. Our experiments show that introducing WAREX leads to significant drops in task success rates, highlighting the limited robustness of state-of-the-art agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinay","familyName":"Venkatesh","name":"Venkatesh, Vinay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamsidhar R","familyName":"Kamanuru","name":"Kamanuru, Vamsidhar R","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lav","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Lav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Kothari","name":"Kothari, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Edge-FIT: Federated Instruction Tuning of Quantized LLMs for Privacy-Preserving Smart Home Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T20:06:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes Edge-FIT (Federated Instruction Tuning on the Edge), a scalable framework for Federated Instruction Tuning (FIT) of Large Language Models (LLMs). Traditional Federated Learning (TFL) methods, like FedAvg, fail when confronted with the massive parameter size of LLMs [3], [6]. Our Edge-FIT framework combines federated learning with 4-bit Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation (QLORA), mitigating the core issues of communication and computational overhead. We demonstrate this by filtering the general-purpose Databricks Dolly 15k dataset for the IoT domain. Experimental results show the Edge-FIT tuned Llama 2(7B) achieves an F1-Score of 0.89. We also demonstrate a viable trade-off using the 3.8B Phi-3-mini model, validating Edge-FIT as a scalable framework for decentralized LLM deployment on home compute gateways."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MACE: A Hybrid LLM Serving System with Colocated SLO-aware Continuous Retraining Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T18:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) deployed on edge servers are increasingly used in latency-sensitive applications such as personalized assistants, recommendation, and content moderation. However, the non-stationary nature of user data necessitates frequent retraining, which introduces a fundamental tension between inference latency and model accuracy under constrained GPU resources. Existing retraining strategies either delay model updates, over-commit resources to retraining, or overlook iteration-level retraining granularity. In this paper, we identify that iteration-level scheduling is crucial for adapting retraining frequency to model drift without violating service-level objectives (SLOs). We propose MACE, a hybrid LLM system that colocates concurrent inference (prefill, decode) and fine-tuning, with intelligent memory management to maximize task performance while promising inference throughput. MACE leverages the insight that not all model updates equally affect output alignment and allocates GPU cycles accordingly to balance throughput, latency, and update freshness. Our trace-driven evaluation shows that MACE matches or exceeds continuous retraining while reducing inference latency by up to 63% and maintaining throughput under resource constraints. Compared to periodic retraining, MACE improves latency breakdown across prefill, decode, and finetune stages, and sustains GPU utilization above 85% in NVIDIA AGX Orin. These results demonstrate that iteration-level hybrid scheduling is a promising direction for deploying LLMs with continual learning capabilities on edge platforms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhas","familyName":"Nair","name":"Nair, Vibhas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amrithaa Ashok","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Amrithaa Ashok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jayvart","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Jayvart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Lagasse","name":"Lagasse, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discovering Transformer Circuits via a Hybrid Attribution and Pruning Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T18:34:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Interpreting language models often involves circuit analysis, which aims to identify sparse subnetworks, or circuits, that accomplish specific tasks. Existing circuit discovery algorithms face a fundamental trade-off: attribution patching is fast but unfaithful to the full model, while edge pruning is faithful but computationally expensive. This research proposes a hybrid attribution and pruning (HAP) framework that uses attribution patching to identify a high-potential subgraph, then applies edge pruning to extract a faithful circuit from it. We show that HAP is 46\\% faster than baseline algorithms without sacrificing circuit faithfulness. Furthermore, we present a case study on the Indirect Object Identification task, showing that our method preserves cooperative circuit components (e.g. S-inhibition heads) that attribution patching methods prune at high sparsity. Our results show that HAP could be an effective approach for improving the scalability of mechanistic interpretability research to larger models. Our code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/HAP-circuit-discovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Mechanistic Interpretability (Mechinterp) and the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on New Perspectives in Graph Machine Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03279","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03279","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03279","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Youjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangjingyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yangjingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MemMamba: Rethinking Memory Patterns in State Space Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T14:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the explosive growth of data, long-sequence modeling has become increasingly important in tasks such as natural language processing and bioinformatics. However, existing methods face inherent trade-offs between efficiency and memory. Recurrent neural networks suffer from gradient vanishing and explosion, making them hard to scale. Transformers can model global dependencies but are constrained by quadratic complexity. Recently, selective state-space models such as Mamba have demonstrated high efficiency with O(n) time and O(1) recurrent inference, yet their long-range memory decays exponentially. In this work, we conduct mathematical derivations and information-theoretic analysis to systematically uncover the memory decay mechanism of Mamba, answering a fundamental question: what is the nature of Mamba's long-range memory and how does it retain information? To quantify key information loss, we further introduce horizontal-vertical memory fidelity metrics that capture degradation both within and across layers. Inspired by how humans distill and retain salient information when reading long documents, we propose MemMamba, a novel architectural framework that integrates state summarization mechanism together with cross-layer and cross-token attention, which alleviates long-range forgetting while preserving linear complexity. MemMamba achieves significant improvements over existing Mamba variants and Transformers on long-sequence benchmarks such as PG19 and Passkey Retrieval, while delivering a 48% speedup in inference efficiency. Both theoretical analysis and empirical results demonstrate that MemMamba achieves a breakthrough in the complexity-memory trade-off, offering a new paradigm for ultra-long sequence modeling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03279","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linxin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Linxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Akang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"QuadEnhancer: Leveraging Quadratic Transformations to Enhance Deep Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T08:35:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The combination of linear transformations and non-linear activation functions forms the foundation of most modern deep neural networks, enabling them to approximate highly complex functions. This paper explores the introduction of quadratic transformations to further increase nonlinearity in neural networks, with the aim of enhancing the performance of existing architectures. To reduce parameter complexity and computational complexity, we propose a lightweight quadratic enhancer that uses low-rankness, weight sharing, and sparsification techniques. For a fixed architecture, the proposed approach introduces quadratic interactions between features at every layer, while only adding negligible amounts of additional model parameters and forward computations. We conduct a set of proof-of-concept experiments for the proposed method across three tasks: image classification, text classification, and fine-tuning large-language models. In all tasks, the proposed approach demonstrates clear and substantial performance gains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Limin","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Limin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiujun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDQ-LLM: Sigma-Delta Quantization for 1-bit LLMs of any size"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T14:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) face significant computational and memory challenges, making extremely low-bit quantization crucial for their efficient deployment. In this work, we introduce SDQ-LLM: Sigma-Delta Quantization for 1-bit LLMs of any size, a novel framework that enables extremely low-bit quantization of LLMs while preserving their linguistic reasoning capabilities. A distinctive feature of SDQ-LLM is the continuous adjustability of the Over-Sampling Ratio (OSR), enabling dynamic adaptation to memory or VRAM constraints by selecting fractional OSR (e.g. 2.5 times) for an optimal trade-off between model size and accuracy. SDQ-LLM uses upsampling combined with Sigma-Delta Quantizer to binarize or ternarize LLMs weights, encoding high-precision parameters into 1-bit or 1.58-bit representations, replacing the multiplication operations within linear layers with addition. This approach significantly enhances inference efficiency under extremely low-bit quantization. To further reduce the loss of quantization precision, we incorporate Hadamard-based weight smoothing prior to quantization, improving the stability and robustness of the weight representations. Furthermore, to fully leverage the continuity of the OSR and reduce precision loss, recognizing the correlation between quantization sensitivity and weight variance, we propose a fine-grained, layer- and linear-wise OSR allocation strategy, MultiOSR. This strategy distributes OSR both across layers and within each layer, based on weight variance and parameter scale. Finally, extensive experiments on OPT and LLaMA model families demonstrate that SDQ-LLM achieves a more efficient and high-precision performance even under highly aggressive low-OSR settings. Our code is available at https://github.com/Dreamlittlecat/LLM-Quant-Factory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiteng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Haotong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quant-dLLM: Post-Training Extreme Low-Bit Quantization for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T13:50:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs), which offer bidirectional context and flexible masked-denoising generation, are emerging as a compelling alternative to autoregressive (AR) LLMs. However, like AR LLMs, their model sizes continue to grow, motivating weight compression for deployment. Although post-training quantization (PTQ) is effective for AR LLMs, directly transferring it to dLLMs at 2-bit leads to unsatisfactory performance. To tackle these challenges, we propose Quant-dLLM, an ultra-low-bit PTQ framework tailored to dLLMs. Since masked-denoising activations in dLLMs differ from the fully visible signals assumed by standard PTQ methods, we introduce Masked Calibration Simulation (MCS) to align calibration with the timestep-dependent masking, which yields more reliable calibrations. Moreover, we propose a Data-aware Any-order Quantizer (DAQ) that learns ultra-low-bit weight representations via an optimization algorithm. It performs iterative approximation guided by our simulated calibration data. In addition, under a strict 2-bit budget, we introduce Adaptive Blockwise Mixed Precision (ABMP), a sensitivity-based precision allocation scheme that adaptively assigns bit width across channel groups. When restricted to 2-bit precision, Quant-dLLM consistently achieves higher accuracy than state-of-the-art (SOTA) AR-transfer PTQ methods on dLLMs. The code and models will be available at: https://github.com/ZTA2785/Quant-dLLM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jielin","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jielin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shelby","familyName":"Heinecke","name":"Heinecke, Shelby","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio","familyName":"Savarese","name":"Savarese, Silvio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CoDA: Coding LM via Diffusion Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T05:41:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models promise bidirectional context and infilling capabilities that autoregressive coders lack, yet practical systems remain heavyweight. We introduce CoDA, a 1.7B-parameter diffusion coder trained on TPU with a fully open-source training pipeline. CoDA pairs large-scale diffusion pre-training with code-centric mid-training and instruction tuning, enabling confidence-guided sampling that keeps inference latency competitive. On Humaneval, MBPP, and EvalPlus, CoDA-1.7B-Instruct matches or surpasses diffusion models up to 7B parameters. Our release includes model checkpoints, evaluation harnesses, and TPU training pipelines to accelerate research on lightweight diffusion-based coding assistants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03264","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03264","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03264","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syeda Nahida","familyName":"Akter","name":"Akter, Syeda Nahida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrimai","familyName":"Prabhumoye","name":"Prabhumoye, Shrimai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Nyberg","name":"Nyberg, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostofa","familyName":"Patwary","name":"Patwary, Mostofa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Shoeybi","name":"Shoeybi, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Catanzaro","name":"Catanzaro, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Front-Loading Reasoning: The Synergy between Pretraining and Post-Training Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T20:08:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The prevailing paradigm for enhancing the reasoning abilities of LLMs revolves around post-training on high-quality, reasoning-intensive data. While emerging literature suggests that reasoning data is increasingly incorporated also during the mid-training stage-a practice that is relatively more proprietary and less openly characterized-the role of such data in pretraining remains unclear. In particular, due to the opaqueness of pretraining corpora in most frontier models, the effect of reasoning data introduced at different phases of pre- and/or post-training is relatively less reported in the scientific literature. This raises several important questions: Is adding reasoning data earlier during pretraining any better than introducing it during post-training? Could earlier inclusion risk overfitting and harm generalization, or instead establish durable foundations that later fine-tuning cannot recover? We conduct the first systematic study of how reasoning data-varying in scale, diversity, and quality-affects LLM performance when introduced at different stages of training. We find that front-loading reasoning data into pretraining is critical (19% avg gain), establishing foundational capabilities that cannot be fully replicated by later-stage SFT, even with more data. We uncover an asymmetric principle for optimal data allocation: pretraining benefits most from broad diversity in reasoning patterns (11% avg gain), while SFT is more sensitive to data quality (15% avg gain). We show that high-quality pretraining data has latent effects, activated only after SFT, and that naively scaling SFT data can be detrimental, washing away the benefits of early reasoning injection. Our results challenge the conventional separation of language modeling and reasoning, providing a principled guide for strategically allocating data across the entire training pipeline to build more capable models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03264","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C.","familyName":"Coelho","name":"Coelho, C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Hohmann","name":"Hohmann, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Fernández","name":"Fernández, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Penter","name":"Penter, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Ihlenfeldt","name":"Ihlenfeldt, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"O.","familyName":"Niggemann","name":"Niggemann, O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-Driven Temperature Modelling of Machine Tools by Neural Networks: A Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"J.2; I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T15:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Thermal errors in machine tools significantly impact machining precision and productivity. Traditional thermal error correction/compensation methods rely on measured temperature-deformation fields or on transfer functions. Most existing data-driven compensation strategies employ neural networks (NNs) to directly predict thermal errors or specific compensation values. While effective, these approaches are tightly bound to particular error types, spatial locations, or machine configurations, limiting their generality and adaptability. In this work, we introduce a novel paradigm in which NNs are trained to predict high-fidelity temperature and heat flux fields within the machine tool. The proposed framework enables subsequent computation and correction of a wide range of error types using modular, swappable downstream components. The NN is trained using data obtained with the finite element method under varying initial conditions and incorporates a correlation-based selection strategy that identifies the most informative measurement points, minimising hardware requirements during inference. We further benchmark state-of-the-art time-series NN architectures, namely Recurrent NN, Gated Recurrent Unit, Long-Short Term Memory (LSTM), Bidirectional LSTM, Transformer, and Temporal Convolutional Network, by training both specialised models, tailored for specific initial conditions, and general models, capable of extrapolating to unseen scenarios. The results show accurate and low-cost prediction of temperature and heat flux fields, laying the basis for enabling flexible and generalisable thermal error correction in machine tool environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangji","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Guangji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StructPrune: Structured Global Pruning asymptotics with $\\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{N})$ GPU Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T19:16:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:00:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pruning is critical for scaling large language models (LLMs). Global pruning achieves strong performance but requires $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ memory, which is infeasible for billion-parameter models. Local pruning reduces GPU memory usage to that of a single layer by pruning layers independently, but it neglects inter-layer dependencies and often leads to suboptimal performance in high-sparsity regimes. Unlike unstructured pruning, structured pruning produces regular sparsity patterns that align well with GPU kernels and library optimizations, making it more hardware-efficient. However, structured pruning typically relies on global pruning, since structured patterns are more prone to severe performance degradation under local optimization. To jointly achieve structured pruning and the memory efficiency of local pruning, we propose a divide-and-conquer strategy that decomposes the global pruning problem into coordinated subproblems across different modules, each of which fits within limited GPU memory. Building on this idea, we design \\textbf{STRUPRUNE}, an ADMM-based framework that integrates structured sparsity into the pruning process, combining the memory efficiency of local pruning with the hardware compatibility of structured methods. We derive a closed-form analytical solution for structured pruning masks that provides an explicit rule for layer-wise sparsity allocation, and further develop an energy-based asymptotic framework yielding a softmax-form allocation scheme that simplifies optimization while adapting to heterogeneous layer importance. Experiments demonstrate that STRUPRUNE matches the perplexity of global structured pruning while reducing memory cost from $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(\\sqrt{N})$, enabling practical deployment at the billion-parameter scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:45:13Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:45:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:45:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":372,"totalPages":4,"page":4},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D%20AND%20(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20%22mixture%20of%20experts%22)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=4"}}